From 428d43ba6d0ab4c9a0c55a07a862220d7c3e94f8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 01:37:11 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [DVBridge] Low-Latency mapped to the black the display gives

The Low-Latency target of a display is what its Dolby Vision block gives
for it: a peak, primaries and a black. Hades took the peak and the
primaries and kept the black of the film; a black could only be set by
hand, with the peak and colours ("Low-Latency: peak and colours set
here"). The black is now taken from the block too, where those are not
set by hand, with the black level lift of ITU-R BT.2390 that the black
set by hand already uses: the darkest parts rise to it the most, brighter
ones less, the highlights not at all. Within 0.5 cd/m2, as the black set
by hand. A new expert setting, "Low-Latency: use the black level the
display gives" (videoscreen.hadesdvllblackedid, on), turns it off.

Hades' EDID corpus (177 unique version 2 blocks, 47 version 1 ones):
40 version 2 blocks give 0 (OLED), and nothing changes for them (the
LG C5 among them); the others give a median of 0.035 cd/m2, at most
0.27 (edge-lit LCD TVs of 240 to 370 cd/m2, a contrast of 1 180 to
1 600 to 1 that such panels have); issue #25's projector gives 0.0865.
None comes near the 1.03 cd/m2 that the field allows. A display whose
real black is lower than the one it gives (an LCD TV with local dimming
may give the black of its panel without it) would show grey blacks: the
setting says so.

kodi.log says where the black of the mapping came from. A choice for the
user, made on these figures.
